.TH NOICE-RS 1
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B noice-rs
.RB [ \-ct ]
.RB [ \-f
.RB SAVE_FILE]
.RB [ DIR ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBnoice-rs\fR is a suckless-aligned file browser written in Rust. It provides a minimal, keyboard-driven interface for browsing files and directories with compile-time configuration following the suckless philosophy.
.P
\fBdir\fR defaults to the current working directory when not specified.
Additionally, if \fBdir\fR is a relative path \fBnoice-rs\fR
will not go back beyond the first component of the path using standard
navigation binds.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c
Enables color mode (default: enabled).
.TP
.B \-t  
Enables tilde home display in path.
.TP
.B \-f
Use an alternate save file for session state.
.SH KEYBINDINGS
.TP
\fIk\fR or \fI[Up]\fR
Move to the previous entry.
.TP
\fIj\fR or \fI[Down]\fR
Move to the next entry.
.TP
\fICtrl-U\fR
Scroll up half a page.
.TP
\fICtrl-D\fR
Scroll down half a page.
.TP
\fIgg\fR or \fI[Home]\fR
Move to the first entry.
.TP
\fIG\fR or \fI[End]\fR
Move to the last entry.
.TP
\fIl\fR, \fI[Right]\fR or \fI[Return]\fR
Open current file or directory.
.TP
\fIh\fR, \fI[Left]\fR or \fI[Backspace]\fR
Back up one directory.
.TP
\fI/\fR
Change filter (search within directory).
.TP
\fIc\fR
Change directory (interactive prompt).
.TP
\fIg<key>\fR or \fI'<key>\fR
Jump to directory matching <key> in the config.
.TP
\fI''\fR
Jump to last directory (toggle between current and previous).
.TP
\fInf\fR
Make a new file, creates leading directories if needed.
.TP
\fInd\fR
Make a new directory, creates leading directories if needed.
.TP
\fI[Space]\fR
Toggle mark state on the current entry.
.TP
\fIy\fR
Yank marked entries or the current if none are marked.
.TP
\fIu\fR
Un-yank (clear) previously yanked entries.
.TP
\fIp\fR
Copy yanked entries to the current directory.
.TP
\fIm\fR
Move yanked entries to the current directory.
.TP
\fIL\fR
Link yanked entries to the current directory.
.TP
\fIr\fR
Rename current entry.
.TP
\fIDD\fR
Delete marked entries or the current if none are marked.
.TP
\fIS\fR
Toggle showing file size.
.TP
\fICtrl-H\fR or \fI.\fR
Toggle showing hidden files (.dotfiles).
.TP
\fId\fR
Toggle sorting by directories first.
.TP
\fIt\fR
Toggle sorting by last modified time.
.TP
\fIv\fR
Toggle sorting by version number.
.TP
\fIs\fR
Sort by name.
.TP
\fICtrl-L\fR or \fIR\fR
Force a redraw/reload.
.TP
\fI!\fR
Spawn a shell in current directory.
.TP
\fIz\fR
Spawn system monitor (top).
.TP
\fIe\fR
Open selected entry with the EDITOR or vi.
.TP
\fIM\fR
Open selected entry with media player.
.TP
\fI?\fR
Show this manual page.
.TP
\fIq\fR or \fI[ESC]\fR
Quit noice-rs.
.SH FILTERS
Filters allow you to use pattern matching to display only matched entries
in the current directory view. This enables searching for a particular
entry or entries. Filters do not stack, they are applied individually.
To reset the filter, input an empty filter expression.
.SH CONFIGURATION
\fBnoice-rs\fR is configured by editing the config.rs file and recompiling the source code,
following the suckless philosophy of compile-time configuration.
.P
The following environment variables are respected:
.TP
\fBEDITOR\fR
Used by the \fIe\fR command to edit files (default: vi)
.TP
\fBSHELL\fR 
Used by the \fI!\fR command to spawn shell (default: sh)
.TP
\fBNOICEMP\fR
Used by the \fIM\fR command for media player (default: "mpv --shuffle")
.TP
\fBNOICETOP\fR
Used by the \fIz\fR command for system monitor (default: top)
.TP
\fBNOICEMAN\fR
Used by the \fI?\fR command for manual (default: "man noice")
.SH DIRECTORY JUMPS
The following directory jump shortcuts are available with \fIg<key>\fR or \fI'<key>\fR:
.TP
\fIr\fR
Jump to root directory (/)
.TP
\fIe\fR  
Jump to /etc
.TP
\fIb\fR
Jump to /bin  
.TP
\fIu\fR
Jump to /usr
.TP
\fIm\fR
Jump to /media
.TP
\fI.\fR
Jump to ~/.config
.TP
\fI'\fR
Jump to last directory (toggle)
.SH PERFORMANCE
\fBnoice-rs\fR is designed for performance and minimal resource usage:
.TP
Binary size: ~500KB stripped
.TP  
Memory usage: <5MB for typical directories
.TP
Startup time: <10ms
.TP
Dependencies: Only libc for terminal control
.SH PHILOSOPHY
This implementation follows the suckless philosophy:
- Simplicity over features
- Compile-time configuration  
- Minimal dependencies
- User as developer (edit source and recompile)
- No runtime configuration files
- Clear, readable code
